{{Main|Treaty of Tamsui (1656)}}

The settlement was concluded as the [[Treaty of Tamsui (1656)|Treaty of Tamsui]] of 1656, signed at Tamsui on 18 March by [[Kayatsi]], [[Ng Tek-seng]] and [[Tân Chì-hong]], ratified by the General Assembly of Zong and signed by the emperor on 1 June, and in effect from 1 September.

Hachinawa was partitioned along the [[Great Lurandian Mountain Range]], the north–south range whose western flank forms Sinchew's eastern highlands. Its western territories were merged into the Sinchew Colony as a semi-autonomous province, governed by Kayatsi for his lifetime and thereafter by an administrator selected by the governor of Sinchew. The assembly of Hachinawa was retained and continued to exercise legislative authority within the province, subject to the administrator's veto. Its southern territories were confirmed to Louyang. Its eastern territories were constituted as a protectorate of Zong for the lifetime of Hachatsi, after which they passed to the royal house, which took the name [[Etowah]] and governed them as a separate state.

Under the settlement the Sinchew Colony held about 1,811,932 km² and the royal house's new state of Etowah about 380,037 km². [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]] took the empire's southern territories into a colony that already extended far to the west and south.

The empire that had been the largest polity on the western coast in 1618 ceased to exist. Its territory was divided between the colony, Louyang and the royal house, and the royal house did not continue it under its former name: the state it governed from 1656 was Etowah, and the name of Hachinawa passed out of use with the partition.

About 50,000 Baiduists were executed or displaced in the settlement of the colony. The figure is of the same order as the population of the lake settlements in 1645, and the displacement was concentrated in those districts.

The revolutionary army withdrew to the north-east of Tamsui. In the last years of the war it was in retreat and foraging, and it left the districts it had held for land that neither Hachinawa nor the colony had claimed. It established [[Free Sinchew]] there, a territory of about 19,174 km², and held it until 1715, when it was conquered by the Sinchew Colony in the [[First War of the Isthmus]].

A further area was left unclaimed by the settlement. About 82,595 km² between the colony's frontier and the royal house's remaining territory was assigned to no party in 1656 and remained outside the jurisdiction of either. It is the country of the [[Karawã–Apyãwa]] peoples, and it appears on the maps of the period as unclaimed land.

== Aftermath ==

The war removed Hachinawa as a power on the western coast and made the colony the largest polity in the region. The Sinchew Colony took the empire's western territory and Louyang took its southern, and the boundary between the two colonies was drawn along the [[Great Lurandian Mountain Range]] and the line of the treaty.

The Baiduist population of the colony, which had been the majority since 1610, was reduced by the executions and displacements of 1656 and by the departure of those who joined the revolutionary army. The Tianist minority retained the offices and the land.

